---
// Falcon case study. Figma: "Vodafone Idea — Fixed" (362:219), 1728 × 15973, page fill #fcfbf8.
// Blocks are placed at their design coordinates (see styles/case.css); the order below is reading order for phones.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import NextProject from '../../components/NextProject.astro';
import FalconScreen from '../../components/FalconScreen.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import '../../styles/case.css';

import studioWall from '../../assets/falcon/studio-wall.png';
import monitorSetup from '../../assets/falcon/monitor-setup.png';
import heroEngineers from '../../assets/falcon/hero-engineers.png';
import viLogo from '../../assets/falcon/vi-logo.png';
import context from '../../assets/falcon/context.png';
import dashboard from '../../assets/falcon/dashboard.png';
import research from '../../assets/falcon/research.png';
import ia1 from '../../assets/falcon/ia-1.png';
import ia2 from '../../assets/falcon/ia-2.png';
import ia3 from '../../assets/falcon/ia-3.png';
import decision1 from '../../assets/falcon/decision-1.png';
import decision2 from '../../assets/falcon/decision-2.png';
import decision3 from '../../assets/falcon/decision-3.png';

const half = '(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 852px';
---
<Base title="Falcon — Making millions of network events explain a single outage · Suyash" description="Redesigning Falcon, Vodafone Idea's network operations intelligence platform, so engineers can trust, connect and act on information during live outages.">
  <Nav solid />
  <main class="canvas falcon-page" style="height: calc(16390 * var(--u));">

    <!-- ===== Hero (MacBook Pro 16' - 11): red band, cream title card, the engineers at their monitor ===== -->
    <span class="bleed hero-band" style="--y: 0; --h: 1115;" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <!-- ===== The stage: the hero's picture, built from its Figma layers (wall, desk shadow, monitor on its desk, the two
         engineers) with the live Falcon screen on the monitor. While you scroll it holds: the engineers leave as the hero
         gives way to Context, then the view pushes into the monitor until the screen fills the window, and "See in
         motion" starts it (scripts/case-motion.ts, data-stage). Without motion it is simply the hero's picture. ===== -->
    <div class="at stage" style="--x: 0; --y: 0; --w: 1728; --h: 1115;" data-stage data-stage-hold="700">
      <div class="stage__art">
        <Image class="art art--wall" src={studioWall} alt="" widths={[859, 1075, 1718]} sizes="(max-width: 1199px) 1px, 100vw" loading="eager" />
        <span class="art art--shadow"></span>
        <Image class="art art--setup" src={monitorSetup} alt="" widths={[1023, 1448]} sizes="(max-width: 1199px) 1px, 1023px" loading="eager" />
        <div class="stage__screen fs-host" aria-hidden="true" inert><FalconScreen id="falcon-live" /></div>
        <Image class="art art--engineers" src={heroEngineers} alt="" widths={[1194, 1448]} sizes="(max-width: 1199px) 1px, 1194px" loading="eager" data-stage-leave />
      </div>
      <button class="stage__play" type="button" data-falcon-play="falcon-live"><span></span>See in motion</button>
    </div>

    <header class="at hero" style="--x: 0; --y: 0; --w: 1728; --h: 1115;">

      <div class="hero__card" data-reveal>
        <Image class="hero__logo" src={viLogo} alt="Vodafone Idea" widths={[104]} sizes="52px" />
        <h1 class="hero__title t-serif">Making millions of network events explain a <em>single outage.</em></h1>
        <div class="hero__foot">
        <p class="hero__meta c-label"><span>Falcon · Vodafone Idea</span><span>Product Design Intern · Jun–Jul 2026</span></p>
        <p class="hero__summary c-lead">Engineers investigating live outages couldn't tell what data was loaded, what was missing, or how evidence across five systems connected together.</p>
        <!-- the research paper lives under /work/falcon/, so the same lock (middleware.js) guards it -->
        <a class="hero__paper c-label" href="/work/falcon/research-paper.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the research paper (PDF, 8 pages)</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hero__facts" data-stage-leave>
        <span class="hero__line"></span>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Outcome</dt><dd>Evidence for an outage correlated in under 10 seconds, down from a minute</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Users</dt><dd>NOC engineers, network engineers, incident managers, IP leadership</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <span class="hero__line"></span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Motion (scripts/case-motion.ts, Figma "phase 2" notes): data-wipe / data-slide = "image wipes and slides",
         data-stage = "zoom in parallax", data-sticky = "sticky image", data-morph = "re-type text", done as a morph. -->

    <!-- ===== Context: the image wipes in, the text slides in ===== -->
    <h2 class="at c-h2" data-n="01" style="--x: 104; --y: 1400; --w: 492;" data-slide="left">Context</h2>
    <div class="at stack" style="--x: 104; --y: 1484; --w: 492; --gap: 24;" data-slide="left">
      <div class="c-body">
      <p>When a telecom outage occurs, engineers need to understand what's happening before the impact spreads further. Falcon is the platform they use to investigate incidents, trace affected infrastructure, and determine the fastest path to resolution. It brings together signals from multiple network systems, devices, and monitoring tools into a single workspace.</p>
      <p>The challenge wasn't collecting information. Falcon already had access to vast amounts of operational data. The challenge was helping engineers trust what they were seeing, connect evidence across multiple sources, and make decisions confidently while an incident was still unfolding.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="c-quote">This project focused on reducing the gap between information being available and information being understandable.</p>
      <!-- the project at a glance: what I was, when, what I did, and where it ended up -->
      <dl class="glance">
      <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Product Design Intern</dd></div>
      <div><dt>When</dt><dd>June to July 2026, Hyderabad</dd></div>
      <div><dt>My part</dt><dd>Research, information architecture, interface design</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Shipped, and measured in production</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
    <div class="at c-img" style="--x: 876; --y: 1115; --w: 852; --h: 1117;" data-wipe="right" data-stage-hide>
      <Image src={context} alt="An engineer at a desk reviewing incident data on a monitor" widths={[852, 1075]} sizes={half} data-parallax="4" />
    </div>

    <!-- ===== The dashboard (MacBook Pro 16' - 27). With motion the stage fills this screen with the live Falcon; without
         it (phones, reduced motion) the picture stays, and "See in motion" opens the live screen in a dialog. ===== -->
    <div class="bleed c-img dashboard" style="--y: 2232; --h: 1117;" data-stage-hide>
      <Image class="dashboard__screen" src={dashboard} alt="The redesigned Falcon dashboard: Root Cause Table, active tickets and a recommended investigation" widths={[1440, 1672]} sizes="100vw" />
      <button class="stage__play dashboard__play" type="button" data-falcon-open><span></span>See in motion</button>
    </div>
    <dialog class="falcon-dialog" data-falcon-dialog aria-label="Falcon, live">
      <button class="falcon-dialog__close" type="button" data-falcon-close>Close ✕</button>
      <div class="falcon-dialog__screen fs-host"><FalconScreen id="falcon-dialog" /></div>
      <p class="falcon-dialog__hint">Turn your phone sideways for a bigger screen.</p>
    </dialog>

    <!-- ===== The Problem: the red block wipes across, the text slides in ===== -->
    <span class="at red-block" style="--x: 0; --y: 3349; --w: 876; --h: 1129;" aria-hidden="true" data-wipe="left"></span>
    <div class="at stack" style="--x: 1005; --y: 3634; --w: 491; --gap: 30;" data-slide="right">
      <h2 class="c-h2" data-n="02">The problem</h2>
      <div class="c-body stack" style="--gap: 21;">
        <p>Falcon processed vast amounts of operational data, but engineers still spent the first minutes of every incident determining whether they could trust what they were seeing. Information arrived from multiple systems at different speeds, relationships between sources were hidden behind separate views, and AI-generated answers required independent verification. The cost wasn't just slower investigations. It was a gradual loss of trust in the tool itself.</p>
        <p>Over time, engineers adapted by building workarounds. They kept raw logs open in separate tabs, manually stitched evidence together, and treated Falcon as a starting point rather than a source of truth.</p>
        <p class="c-quote">The product had become an unreliable source of truth, so users created their own.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <span class="at c-rule" style="--x: 1005; --y: 4240; --w: 488;"></span>

    <!-- ===== Understanding the System: the image stays while the three methods scroll past; it turns to each one's panel ===== -->
    <div class="at c-img research" style="--x: 0; --y: 4447; --w: 876; --h: 1136;" data-sticky="research" data-room="950">
      <Image src={research} alt="Research: contextual shadowing, user interviews and operational analysis" widths={[876, 1075]} sizes={half} />
      <span class="research__veil research__veil--1" data-frame></span>
      <span class="research__veil research__veil--2" data-frame></span>
      <span class="research__veil research__veil--3" data-frame></span>
    </div>
    <!-- one flowing column, so the intro can grow (its text keeps a readable minimum size) without running into the methods -->
    <div class="at stack" style="--x: 1005; --y: 4579; --w: 490;" data-slide="right">
    <h2 class="c-h2" data-n="03" style="margin-bottom: calc(88 * var(--u));">Understanding the system</h2>
    <p class="c-body" style="margin-bottom: calc(32 * var(--u));">To understand why engineers had stopped trusting Falcon, I approached the problem from three angles: observing live incidents, interviewing users, and analyzing operational data. Each revealed a different part of the story.</p>
    <div class="methods">
      <div style="--gap: 12;" data-step="research"><h3 class="c-h3">Contextual shadowing</h3><p class="c-body">Live incidents revealed a gap between what engineers said and what they did. Engineers routinely waited for data, cross-checked results against raw logs, and relied on workarounds that had become part of their normal workflow.</p></div>
      <div style="--gap: 31;" data-step="research"><h3 class="c-h3">User interviews</h3><p class="c-body">Interviews across roles showed that trust meant different things to different users, but everyone depended on the same underlying information to make decisions.</p></div>
      <div style="--gap: 12;" data-step="research"><h3 class="c-h3">Operational analysis</h3><p class="c-body">Usage data confirmed the pattern: engineers spent nearly ten minutes gathering context by hand before diagnosis could begin, while critical information remained fragmented across multiple sources.</p></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <span class="at c-rule" style="--x: 1005; --y: 5466; --w: 488;"></span>

    <!-- ===== Key Findings → Design Principles: one scene with the same layout. It holds while you scroll; the right side
         morphs from the findings into the principles (headings blur into each other, body lines hand over) and the left side slides across. (Without motion, and on
         phones, the two simply follow each other: the principles sit a screen below, at 1117.) ===== -->
    <div class="at morph" style="--x: 0; --y: 5773; --w: 1728; --h: 2234;" data-morph>
      <div class="at stack" style="--x: 104; --y: 265; --w: 346; --gap: 42;" data-slide="left" data-morph-left="a">
        <h2 class="c-h2" data-n="04">Key findings</h2>
        <p class="c-body">Research surfaced 18 issues, but most were symptoms of a smaller set of underlying problems. Three root causes consistently appeared across observations, interviews, and operational data.</p>
      </div>
      <span class="at c-vrule" style="--x: 852; --y: 0; --h: 737;"></span>
      <h3 class="at c-h3" style="--x: 1005; --y: 48; --w: 290;" data-slide="right" data-morph-item="a">Trust in system state</h3>
      <p class="at c-body" style="--x: 1005; --y: 138; --w: 290;" data-slide="right" data-morph-item="a">Engineers couldn't reliably tell whether data was complete, still loading, or outdated. As a result, they spent time verifying information before acting on it.</p>
      <h3 class="at c-h3" style="--x: 1334; --y: 48; --w: 290;" data-slide="right" data-morph-item="a">The cost of correlation</h3>
      <p class="at c-body" style="--x: 1334; --y: 99; --w: 290;" data-slide="right" data-morph-item="a">Critical evidence was distributed across tickets, logs, topology, and configuration data. Falcon stored the information, but engineers were responsible for connecting it into a coherent explanation.</p>
      <h3 class="at c-h3" style="--x: 1005; --y: 322; --w: 290;" data-slide="right" data-morph-item="a">No explainable evidence</h3>
      <p class="at c-body" style="--x: 1005; --y: 412; --w: 290;" data-slide="right" data-morph-item="a">The AI assistant operated separately from the evidence engineers were already reviewing. Answers required additional verification, creating friction and reducing trust.</p>
      <p class="at c-quote" style="--x: 1334; --y: 322; --w: 290;" data-slide="right" data-morph-item="a">Together, these findings revealed that Falcon's challenge wasn't intelligence. It was helping engineers trust, connect, and act on information during live incidents.</p>

      <div class="at stack" style="--x: 104; --y: 1362.5; --w: 346; --gap: 42;" data-reveal data-morph-left="b">
        <h2 class="c-h2" data-n="05">Design principles</h2>
        <p class="c-body">Three root causes became four principles. Every design decision that follows was evaluated against these rules.</p>
      </div>
      <span class="at c-vrule" style="--x: 852; --y: 1117; --h: 737;" data-morph-hide></span>
      <h3 class="at c-h3" style="--x: 1005; --y: 1129.5; --w: 290;" data-reveal data-morph-item="b">Show information as soon as it exists</h3>
      <p class="at c-body" style="--x: 1005; --y: 1219.5; --w: 290;" data-reveal data-morph-item="b">Engineers should never wait for data the system has already retrieved. Information should appear progressively as it becomes available.</p>
      <h3 class="at c-h3" style="--x: 1334; --y: 1129.5; --w: 290;" data-reveal data-morph-item="b">Never let incomplete look complete</h3>
      <p class="at c-body" style="--x: 1334; --y: 1258.5; --w: 290;" data-reveal data-morph-item="b">Loading, loaded, and stale states must be visually distinct so engineers can immediately understand the reliability of what they're seeing.</p>
      <h3 class="at c-h3" style="--x: 1005; --y: 1418.5; --w: 290;" data-reveal data-morph-item="b">Organize around investigations, not data sources</h3>
      <p class="at c-body" style="--x: 1005; --y: 1547.5; --w: 290;" data-reveal data-morph-item="b">Engineers think in terms of tickets, devices, and incidents, not logs, topology, or monitoring systems. The interface should reflect that mental model.</p>
      <h3 class="at c-h3" style="--x: 1334; --y: 1418.5; --w: 290;" data-reveal data-morph-item="b">Trust requires evidence</h3>
      <p class="at c-body" style="--x: 1334; --y: 1508.5; --w: 290;" data-reveal data-morph-item="b">AI-generated answers should always provide a clear path back to the underlying evidence. Confidence alone is not enough.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- ===== Reframing the Information Architecture: the image stays and fades into the next as each part of the text arrives ===== -->
    <div class="at stack" style="--x: 104; --y: 8054; --w: 497; --gap: 38;" data-slide="left">
      <h2 class="c-h2" data-n="06">Reframing the information architecture</h2>
      <div>
        <h3 class="c-h3 ia-sub">From source-based navigation to investigation-based navigation</h3>
        <div class="c-body ia-copy">
          <p>Falcon was originally organized around where data came from: tickets, logs, topology, and configuration changes. While this mirrored the underlying systems, it didn't reflect how engineers actually investigated incidents. Engineers don't start with a source. They start with a question.</p>
          <p>A typical investigation required moving across multiple tabs and manually stitching information together. The platform stored the evidence, but the burden of correlation remained with the engineer.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="at c-img" style="--x: 1005; --y: 8054; --w: 520; --h: 680;" data-sticky="ia" data-sticky-start="center center"><Image src={ia1} alt="An engineer surrounded by separate monitors, one per data source" widths={[520, 1040]} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 520px" /></div>
    <div class="at stack" style="--x: 104; --y: 9128; --w: 491; --gap: 28;" data-slide="left" data-step="ia">
      <h3 class="c-h3">The shift</h3>
      <div class="c-body">
        <p>Instead of organizing Falcon around data sources, I reorganized it around the unit of investigation: a ticket, device, or IP address. Searching a single identifier now pulls together all related evidence across systems into one continuous workflow.</p>
        <p>This reduced the need to navigate between sources and moved the responsibility for correlation from the engineer to the system.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="at c-img" style="--x: 1005; --y: 8894; --w: 520; --h: 680;" data-sticky-join="ia"><Image src={ia2} alt="Engineers working through a single investigation on one screen" widths={[520, 1040]} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 520px" /></div>
    <div class="at stack" style="--x: 104; --y: 9961; --w: 491; --gap: 20;" data-slide="left" data-step="ia">
      <h3 class="c-h3">A key decision</h3>
      <div class="c-body">
        <p>An early concept explored separate experiences for different user roles. However, incidents frequently move across analysts, engineers, and managers. Role-based views fragmented the investigation and removed critical context during escalations.</p>
        <p>Instead, I designed a single shared surface with layered information density, allowing different roles to access the same incident while engaging at different levels of depth.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="at c-img" style="--x: 1005; --y: 9734; --w: 520; --h: 680;" data-sticky-join="ia"><Image src={ia3} alt="Analysts, engineers and a manager sharing one incident view" widths={[520, 1040]} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 520px" /></div>
    <div class="at stack" style="--x: 104; --y: 10574; --w: 470; --gap: 32;" data-slide="left" data-alone>
      <h3 class="c-h3">Outcome</h3>
      <p class="c-body">The redesign transformed Falcon from a collection of disconnected sources into a unified investigation workflow, creating the foundation for every subsequent design decision.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- ===== Design Decisions 01–03 share a layout: the image on the right stays and fades from one decision to the next while the text scrolls ===== -->
    <div class="at stack dd-col" style="--x: 104; --y: 11110; --w: 362;" data-slide="left">
    <h2 class="c-h2" data-n="07 · Decision 01" style="margin-bottom: calc(24 * var(--u));">Making system state visible</h2>
    <div class="c-body" style="margin-bottom: calc(32 * var(--u));">
      <p>One of the most expensive moments in an outage investigation wasn't diagnosis, it was uncertainty. Engineers often couldn't tell whether they were looking at complete information, partially loaded results, or outdated data. As a result, they routinely waited for all sources to load or manually verified information before acting.</p>
      <p>The underlying issue was that Falcon treated data as a finished object. In reality, information arrived from multiple systems at different speeds, but the interface gave little indication of that state.</p>
    </div>
    <h3 class="c-h3" style="margin-bottom: calc(8 * var(--u));">The decision</h3>
    <div class="c-body">
      <p>Instead of waiting for all sources to return before displaying results, Falcon now reveals information progressively as it becomes available. Every section clearly communicates whether its data is loading, loaded, or stale.</p>
      <p>This shifted the interface from hiding uncertainty to communicating it.</p>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="at c-img" style="--x: 876; --y: 10934; --w: 852; --h: 1117;" data-sticky="dd"><Image src={decision1} alt="Falcon showing loading, loaded and stale states for each section" widths={[852, 1267]} sizes={half} /></div>
    <aside class="at c-why" style="--x: 1165; --y: 11721; --w: 459; --h: 282;" data-slide="right">
      <h3 class="c-h3">Why it matters</h3>
      <p class="c-body">Trust isn't created when a system has all the answers. It's created when a system is honest about what it knows and what it doesn't. By making system state visible, engineers could begin investigating immediately instead of waiting for certainty before taking action.</p>
    </aside>

    <!-- ===== Design Decision 02 ===== -->
    <div class="at stack dd-col" style="--x: 104; --y: 12211; --w: 362;" data-slide="left" data-step="dd">
    <h2 class="c-h2" data-n="08 · Decision 02" style="margin-bottom: calc(24 * var(--u));">Reducing the cost of correlation</h2>
    <div class="c-body" style="margin-bottom: calc(32 * var(--u));">
      <p>Even when Falcon contained the right information, engineers still had to assemble the story themselves. Investigating a single incident often meant moving between tickets, logs, topology maps, configuration changes, and AI outputs to understand how seemingly disconnected signals were related.</p>
      <p>The platform stored the evidence, but the burden of correlation remained with the user.</p>
    </div>
    <h3 class="c-h3" style="margin-bottom: calc(8 * var(--u));">The decision</h3>
    <div class="c-body">
      <p>This is where the new architecture becomes a screen. Searching a ticket, device, or IP address opens one investigation workspace, with the evidence from every system already gathered around it and each source keeping its own loading state.</p>
      <p>The responsibility for connecting information shifted from the engineer to the system.</p>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="at c-img" style="--x: 876; --y: 12051; --w: 852; --h: 1117;" data-sticky-join="dd"><Image src={decision2} alt="An investigation workspace pulling evidence from several systems together" widths={[852, 1096]} sizes={half} /></div>
    <aside class="at c-why" style="--x: 1165; --y: 12838; --w: 459; --h: 282;" data-slide="right">
      <h3 class="c-h3">Why it matters</h3>
      <p class="c-body">During outages, engineers aren't trying to navigate tools. They're trying to answer questions. By surfacing related evidence in one place, Falcon reduced context switching and allowed engineers to focus on understanding the incident rather than gathering information about it.</p>
    </aside>

    <!-- ===== Design Decision 03 (MacBook Pro 16' - 24) ===== -->
    <div class="at c-img" style="--x: 872; --y: 13168; --w: 856; --h: 1117;" data-sticky-join="dd"><Image src={decision3} alt="The Recommended Investigation card with the reasons behind it" widths={[856, 1096]} sizes={half} /></div>
    <div class="at stack dd3 dd-col" style="--x: 104; --y: 13168; --w: 362; --h: 1117; --gap: 38;" data-slide="left" data-step="dd">
      <h2 class="c-h2" data-n="09 · Decision 03">Reducing triage effort</h2>
      <div class="stack" style="--gap: 10;">
        <div class="c-body">
          <p>As Falcon became faster and easier to navigate, a new problem emerged.</p>
          <p>On high-volume days, engineers weren't struggling to find information, they were struggling to decide where to start. Hundreds of open tickets could appear simultaneously, each competing for attention with little indication of which incident mattered most.</p>
          <p>A faster dashboard still presented a wall of equally urgent problems.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="stack" style="--gap: 17;">
        <h3 class="c-h3">The decision</h3>
        <div class="c-body">
          <p>Instead of asking engineers to manually triage every ticket, Falcon now performs an initial prioritization pass and surfaces a single Recommended Investigation.</p>
          <p>Every open ticket is scored against a transparent set of operational signals:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Priority level</li>
            <li>SLA proximity</li>
            <li>Shared upstream infrastructure</li>
            <li>Multi-circle impact</li>
            <li>Configuration changes</li>
            <li>Customer impact</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The highest-scoring incident is surfaced alongside the specific reasons it earned that recommendation.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <aside class="at c-why" style="--x: 1165; --y: 14144; --w: 459; --h: 282;" data-slide="right">
      <h3 class="c-h3">Why it matters</h3>
      <p class="c-body">The recommendation turned Falcon from a passive monitoring tool into an active investigation partner. And because every recommendation shows its reasons, engineers can check it instead of taking it on faith.</p>
    </aside>

    <!-- ===== Measuring Success ===== -->
    <h2 class="at c-h2" data-n="10" style="--x: 104; --y: 14424; --w: 492;" data-reveal>Measuring success</h2>
    <div class="at stack" style="--x: 104; --y: 14521; --w: 643; --gap: 24;" data-reveal>
      <p class="c-body">The redesign was ultimately evaluated on a simple question:</p>
      <p class="c-quote">Could engineers reach confident decisions faster during live incidents?</p>
      <div class="c-body" style="max-width: calc(491 * var(--u));">
      <p>Two clocks changed after deployment. The system's: Falcon now returns the correlated evidence for an incident in under ten seconds, down from about a minute. And the engineer's: the time from first alert to action fell by 25%.</p>
      <p>The impact extended beyond speed. By making system state visible, consolidating evidence into a single workflow, and surfacing explainable recommendations, Falcon became a more trusted source of operational truth during outages.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ===== Outcomes, drawn: the two measured results as figures (type and hairlines, like the rest of the page), then
         the qualitative outcomes as a numbered row. With motion the marks draw in as the band arrives. ===== -->
    <section class="at metrics" style="--x: 104; --y: 15040; --w: 1520;" aria-label="Outcomes" data-reveal>
      <header class="metrics__head"><span>Outcomes</span><span>After deployment</span></header>
      <div class="metrics__figs">
        <figure class="metric">
          <p class="metric__num" aria-hidden="true">25<span>%</span></p>
          <figcaption class="c-lead">less time to resolve an incident, from first alert to action</figcaption>
